  "kind": "period",
  "title": "Islamic Golden Age",
  "summary": "Under the Abbasid caliphs, who took power in 750, Baghdad became a centre of learning. Between 750 and 1000, nearly all the Greek works available in the Byzantine Empire and the Middle East were translated into Arabic, many at the House of Wisdom. Harun al-Rashid's reign (786–809) is taken as the start of the golden age of learning. Abbasid rule ended when the Mongols destroyed Baghdad in 1258. These translations later helped fuel the European Renaissance.",
